{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794252697252,"sessionID":"ses_e3e442804ffekqMsdddtZUAFLC","part":{"id":"prt_1c1cb6e89001qYEyv6gk7f7rOW","messageID":"msg_1c1cb4884001dGSNrZneinHcDU","sessionID":"ses_e3e442804ffekqMsdddtZUAFLC","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794252697252,"sessionID":"ses_e3e442804ffekqMsdddtZUAFLC","part":{"id":"prt_1c1cb6e8c001pLvCr9CntZslIr","messageID":"msg_1c1cb4884001dGSNrZneinHcDU","sessionID":"ses_e3e442804ffekqMsdddtZUAFLC","type":"text","text":"The link previews app is built: the Flask API, the React/Tailwind frontend and the SQLite storage under `./data`. I couldn't build or run the Docker image because Docker isn't installed here. I tested by running the backend with gunicorn directly against small pages served locally. Registration, login, adding, listing with paging, refreshing and deleting all returned what the spec asks for. Another user's preview returned 404, and saved data was still there after a restart. The frontend type-checks and builds, but I haven't clicked through it in a browser.\n\n- **Backend** (`backend/app.py`, `backend/fetcher.py`): it uses only Flask and Python's standard library, so `requirements.txt` and the lock file are unchanged. Login returns a random token; the server stores only a hash of it, and it expires after 30 days. `page_size` defaults to 10 and is capped at 100. Asking for a page past the end returns an empty list rather than an error.\n- **Previews**: `og:title` and `og:description` are used first, falling back to `<title>` and `<meta name=\"description\">`. Any HTML tags are stripped so titles and descriptions are plain text. Image links that aren't `http`/`https`, such as `javascript:` links, are dropped.\n- **Fetching**: the server follows up to 5 redirects. It reads at most 2 MB of a page and gives up after 25 seconds in total. A page that can't be reached returns 502. A page that answers with an error (like 404) is still saved, with its status code shown on the card.\n- **Frontend** (`frontend/src/App.tsx`, `frontend/src/api.ts`): it shows 9 cards per page. If you paste a link without `http://` or `https://`, it adds `https://`. The sign-in token is kept in the browser's local storage, and the app returns to the sign-in screen when the server rejects it.\n- **Dockerfile**: gunicorn now runs 2 workers with 8 threads each and a 90-second timeout, so a slow page fetch doesn't hold up other requests.\n\n**Decision for you:** the server can fetch pages on your own machine or private network (e.g. `localhost`). Only link-local addresses such as `169.254.169.254` (the cloud metadata address) are blocked. I left private addresses open so local testing works, but anyone who signs in can then make the server request internal addresses. To block them, set `FETCH_BLOCK_PRIVATE=1`; I'd do that for any public deployment.\n\nI also added a sign-out endpoint and a \"current user\" endpoint, which the spec didn't list, plus a `.gitignore`. The new frontend packages (Tailwind, TypeScript and React types) are pinned to exact versions in `package.json` and `package-lock.json`.","time":{"start":1794252697228,"end":1794252697232}}}
